From: Joel Groves <happygreentreefrog@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: keep the TID for the RX PN replay check

mt76_check_ccmp_pn() picks the per-TID replay counter from
status->qos_ctl. mt7996_mac_fill_rx() only sets qos_ctl after an early
return that is taken for frames already reordered by hardware RRO
(hw_aggr), so for those frames qos_ctl stays 0 and every TID is checked
against the TID 0 counter. When TID 0 traffic (e.g. video) has advanced
that counter, valid frames on other TIDs (e.g. background, TID 2) are
dropped as replays. Seen as one-way packet loss across a BT8-to-BT8
batman-adv mesh that grows with load.

Set qos_ctl for every QoS data frame before the early return.

---
diff --git a/mt7996/mac.c b/mt7996/mac.c
index f7694faf..976905d4 100644
--- a/mt7996/mac.c
+++ b/mt7996/mac.c
@@ -697,12 +697,19 @@ mt7996_mac_fill_rx(struct mt7996_dev *dev, enum mt76_rxq_id q,
 		}
 	}
 
+	/* The TID is also needed for the per-TID CCMP PN replay check in
+	 * mt76_check_ccmp_pn(), including for frames already reordered by
+	 * hardware RRO. Without it every frame is checked against the TID 0
+	 * counter and lower-priority TIDs are dropped as replays.
+	 */
+	if (ieee80211_is_data_qos(fc))
+		status->qos_ctl = qos_ctl;
+
 	if (!status->wcid || !ieee80211_is_data_qos(fc) || hw_aggr)
 		return 0;
 
 	status->aggr = unicast &&
 		       !ieee80211_is_qos_nullfunc(fc);
-	status->qos_ctl = qos_ctl;
 	status->seqno = IEEE80211_SEQ_TO_SN(seq_ctrl);
 
 	return 0;
